  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nNot much change to the extended forecast, with good agreement\nthrough the end of the week, and differences arising by next\nweekend with the upper pattern. Very wet conditions are still\nfavored along the Continental Divide, focused on the San Juan\nRange.\n\nMonday through Tuesday...persistent southwest flow will prevail\nacross Colorado through the early part of the work week. Weak\nembedded energy will keep persistent shower activity going along\nthe Continental Divide through much of this period. At this\ntime, all shower activity looks to remain west, where the main\nenergy lifts across western Colorado, to the northeast. Very\nweak instability could lead to a couple rumbles of thunder\nMonday and Tuesday afternoons. Snow levels will be high, above\n13 kft, and a dusting of snow may be possible at best. Across\nthe Plains, a weak cold front will arrive by Monday morning,\nwhich will help keep temperatures cool, mainly in the 60s.\nTemperatures will once again warm up on Tuesday, with mostly 70s\nfor the lower elevations.\n\nWednesday through Friday...models still bringing a strong storm\nsystem across the region for mid to late week. There remains\ndifferences in storm track, with the ECMWF furthest south, and\nthe GFS/Canadian north. All guidance and ensembles have a deep\nmoisture tap off the eastern Pacific, and PWAT values will climb\nto near an inch across the region. Showers will increase in\ncoverage along the Continental Divide on Wednesday, peaking in\nintensity Wednesday night into Thursday, and likely linger\nacross the region into Friday. NBM guidance, ensembles and\ndeterministic guidance all have 1 to 3 inches of QPF across the\nSan Juan Range during this period. NBM is centered around 3\ninches, and WPC guidance is in excess of 4 for the days 5-7\nperiod. DESI probs of 2 inches or more are greater than 50% for\nthe San Juan Range. So, overall confidence is higher for a heavy\nrainfall event. As for snow, snow levels will start out high on\nWednesday, above 12-13 kft. They will likely lower towards the\nend of the event, Thursday night into Friday morning, to near 10\nkft. Current snow progs are in the 2-4 inch range for the peaks,\nmainly across the San Juan Range. For the Plains, southwesterly\nflow isn't that conducive for shower activity, and most of the\nPlains east of I-25 look to remain dry. Could a few showers\nspread east off the Sangre de Cristo Range west of I-25, sure,\nbut they will likely not amount to much. Model guidance has\nprecipitation coming to an end by Friday morning as the upper\nsystem shifts northeast away from the area.\n\nTemperatures during this period will remain warm, with 70s to\nlower 80s by Friday. These temperatures will be a few degrees\nshy of records at Colorado Springs and Pueblo.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...uncertainty increases for next weekend\nwith the evolution of the upper pattern. NBM pop grids look\ngood, with low end precipitation chances across the area on\nSaturday, drying out for the weekend. Most guidance does have a\ncold front, with highs mainly in the 60s to near 70 for the\nPlains.",
